## Create

```js
const p = document.createElement("p");
p.textContent = "New paragraph";
```

`p` — new element, not in DOM yet.

## Insert

```js
parent.appendChild(p);
```

`appendChild` — to end.

## Other methods

```js
parent.append(p, text);
parent.prepend(p);
parent.before(p);
parent.after(p);
```

## Remove

```js
el.remove();
parent.removeChild(el);
```

## Building a list

```js
const ul = document.querySelector("#list");
["Milk", "Bread", "Eggs"].forEach(text => {
  const li = document.createElement("li");
  li.textContent = text;
  ul.appendChild(li);
});
```

## Why not innerHTML

`innerHTML +=` recreates everything, loses listeners. `createElement` + `append` — precise.

## What if

- Insert element twice — moved, not copied.
- `appendChild(null)` — TypeError.

## Common mistake

`parent.appendChild("<p>x</p>")` — `appendChild` takes **an element**, not a string. String — via `innerHTML`.

## Summary

- `createElement("p")` — create
- `parent.appendChild(el)` — append
- `el.remove()` — remove
- Not the same as `innerHTML`

